menu "winject-fec"

config WINJECT_FEC_RS_PIE
    bool "Reed-Solomon FEC encode with the PIE SIMD kernel"
    depends on SOC_CPU_HAS_PIE
    default y
    help
        Encode FEC parity with the ESP32-P4 PIE kernel instead of the table path: about 2.8x
        faster (k=21, 10 parity rows, 1403-byte shards: 2.7 ms vs 7.7 ms), which frees about
        16% of core 0 at FEC 21/31.

        Cost: the hardware H.264 encoder shares PSRAM bandwidth with the FEC path, and the
        faster kernel sends each block's parity in a tighter burst. With a parity spread of 0
        that costs about 1 encoder fps at 21/31 (26.4 vs 27.4); with 25 ms or more the
        difference is about 0.1-0.3 fps. Turn it off where every encoder frame matters more
        than core 0 time.

config WINJECT_FEC_TX_PSRAM
    bool "FEC transmit: data row copies in PSRAM"
    depends on SPIRAM
    default y
    help
        Allocate the encoder's copies of a block's data rows (k rows of the largest datagram,
        about 43 KiB at k=31 and 1400 bytes) in PSRAM, keeping internal RAM for the network and
        USB drivers. The PIE kernel reads PSRAM directly.

config WINJECT_FEC_RX_PSRAM
    bool "FEC receive: decode rows in PSRAM"
    depends on SPIRAM
    default n
    help
        Allocate the rows the FEC decoder builds and the payloads it recovers in PSRAM, keeping
        internal RAM for the network and USB drivers.

endmenu
